{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And Jair the son of Manasseh</b> — this man is traced through his mother's family because he took his inheritance together with them, for in the book of Chronicles (I Chronicles 2:21–22) it is explicitly stated that he was the son of Hezron, the son of Perez, the son of Judah, and his mother was a daughter of Machir the son of Manasseh. For it is written: \"And afterward Hezron came to the daughter of Machir the father of Gilead, and he took her, and he was sixty years old, and she bore him Segub, and Segub begot Jair, and he had twenty-three cities in the land of Gilead.\" And it is written: \"And Geshur and Aram took the villages of Jair from them — Kenath and its daughter-towns, sixty cities. All these were the sons of Machir the father of Gilead\" (ibid. v. 23). From this it appears that Nobah, who captured Kenath and its daughter-towns, was a son of Jair, and therefore they too are called the villages of Jair. The reason for \"And Moses gave Gilead to Machir the son of Manasseh\" (Numbers 32:40) — to his family, for his sons captured it. And likewise, \"And to Machir I gave Gilead\" (Deuteronomy 3:15) — to the Machirite, for because he was eminent and head over all his seed, his sons are called by his name, for thus the verses speak of the heads of the tribes in their inheritance: \"southward to Ephraim and northward to Manasseh\" (Joshua 17:10), and likewise, \"To Manasseh belonged the land of Tappuah\" etc. (ibid. v. 8). And what the verse states in the book of Joshua (17:1): \"And the lot was for the tribe of Manasseh, for he was the firstborn of Joseph — to Machir the firstborn of Manasseh, the father of Gilead, for he was a man of war, and he had Gilead and Bashan\" — this too refers to his sons, who were men of war, for they went to Gilead and captured it, and therefore Moses gave them a large portion from them. And perhaps Machir was still alive and the decree of the wilderness had not been decreed upon him, because he was not among those mustered by Moses and Aaron, since he was over sixty years old and lived many years like the earlier generations."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Ramban cites I Chronicles 2:21–23; the phrase 'in the book of Chronicles (א ב כא כב)' expands the abbreviation א as I Chronicles."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:14.465715863Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}